# CHAPTER 4: "DEBTS"

---

The message came three days after Kai's first job with the Surge.

Encrypted line. Unknown sender. Professional formatting—the kind Contractors used when reaching out through the network.

> Kai Reyes. We need to talk. You worked with my brother. I have questions. And a job, if you're interested. Meet me at the Rusty Spoke. Tonight. 2100 hours. Come alone.
> 
> 
> *—V.C.*
> 

Kai stared at the message. Brother. Questions.

His first thought: *Juno.*

His chest tightened. Pulled up his contacts, found Juno's entry. Still there. Hadn't deleted it. Couldn't bring himself to.

The last message between them was three weeks old. Logistics for the Silas Ward job. Professional. Clean. No idea it would be the last.

He closed the contact. Looked at the new message again.

V.C. Vera Cross. Had to be.

Juno had mentioned her once. Older sister. Hard as nails. Took over their father's Contractor network when the old man retired. Juno respected her. Maybe feared her a little.

Now she wanted to talk.

Kai checked the time. 1847 hours. Two hours to decide.

Stitch noticed him staring at his Data Tap. "Trouble?"

"Maybe. Juno's sister wants to meet."

Stitch set down his tea. "You going?"

"Should I?"

"That depends. Can you face her?"

Kai didn't answer. Didn't know if he could.

But he owed Juno that much. Owed his family answers.

"Yeah. I'm going."

---

The Rusty Spoke sat in Edgehaven's industrial quarter—bar for workers, mercs, and people who didn't ask questions. Dim lighting, scratched tables, music low enough to hear your own thoughts. VSD didn't patrol here. Corpos didn't visit. It was neutral ground.

Kai arrived at 2055. Early. Scanned the room with his Vantage Optics—exits marked, threat assessment running, faces cataloged. Old habits.

She was already there.

Corner booth, back to the wall, clear sight lines to the entrance. Professional positioning. Vera Cross looked like her brother—same sharp features, same tired eyes that had seen too much. But harder. Edges worn down to blade-sharpness by years in the life.

Late forties. No visible chrome except a Data Tap and basic optics. Dressed like someone who didn't need augments to be dangerous.

She saw him. Gestured to the seat across from her.

Kai crossed the bar. Sat. Kept his hands visible on the table.

"Kai Reyes." Her voice was controlled. Measured. "You're late."

"I'm five minutes early."

"I've been here for twenty. That makes you late." She signaled the bartender. Two drinks arrived—something amber, probably whiskey. "You worked with my brother. Juno Cross."

"I did."

"Tell me what happened."

Direct. No preamble. Kai appreciated that. Didn't make it easier.

"We took a job. Extraction. Client named Silas Ward—ex-Atlas, wanted for corporate espionage. High risk, but Juno vetted it. Said it was clean."

"It wasn't."

"No. Blackout Division ambushed us mid-extraction. Hit us hard—neural scramblers, stealth tech, military tactics. They were there for Ward, but they didn't care who else died."

Vera's expression didn't change. Just listened.

"Juno was coordinating from outside. They hit him first—took him down before we knew what was happening." Kai's hands tightened on the glass. "By the time I got to him, it was too late. He was... he had seconds."

"What did he say?"

Kai met her eyes. "He told me to tell his daughter he tried to come home."

Vera's jaw tightened. Just slightly. The only crack in her armor.

"His Data Tap was playing 'Happy Birthday.' For Emma. He'd queued it for when the job finished." Kai's voice was quiet. "It kept playing after he died."

Silence. Just the bar's ambient noise. Music, voices, the clink of glasses.

Vera took a drink. Slow. Controlled.

"Dax Vega got you out."

"Yeah. I was hit—spinal damage, lethal. Dax drove me to Stitch's clinic. I survived. Juno didn't."

"And Silas Ward?"

"Sacrificed himself. Drew the ambush away so Dax could extract me. Assumed dead."

Vera processed this. "So my brother died protecting his crew and completing his contract."

"Yes."

"That's the way he'd want it." She finished her drink. "Doesn't make it hurt less. But it's the way."

Kai understood. Contractor code. You died doing the job, you died right.

Didn't mean the family stopped grieving.

"His memorial is at RN," Vera said. "Sector 7, Wall 3, Plaque 47. I'm giving you the Associates password." She pulled up her Data Tap, sent the credentials. "Juno would want you to visit. Pay your respects."

Kai's Data Tap pinged. Password received.

"Emma doesn't know how he died," Vera continued. "Told her it was an accident at work. She's seven. Doesn't need to know about Blackout Division or ambushes or her father bleeding out in an alley."

"Understood."

"Lena—his wife—couldn't stay in their apartment. Too many memories. They moved. I'm taking care of them now." Vera's voice was steel. "Contractor network set up a fund for Emma. Education, future, whatever she needs. That's the code. We take care of our own."

"I'd like to contribute."

"You don't have to."

"I want to."

Vera studied him. Assessing. "You feel guilty."

"Yeah."

"Don't. You didn't kill him. Blackout did." Her eyes were hard. "Guilt doesn't honor the dead. Action does. You want to honor Juno? Keep working. Keep the code. Protect your crew like he protected his."

Kai nodded. "I will."

"Good." Vera leaned back. "Now. Business. I have a job. High-risk data retrieval. Atlas facility, classified servers, need someone who can dive deep and two people who can handle security while she works. You interested?"

"Depends. What's the data?"

"That's need-to-know. Client wants specific files extracted from an Atlas R&D archive. Medical tech division. You get paid to retrieve, not to read."

Medical tech. Surge-adjacent, maybe. Kai kept his expression neutral.

"Who's the Diver?"

"Best in Veyline City. Elena Markovic. Heard of her?"

Kai had. Legends traveled. Elena Markovic—the Ghost. Gridwalker so good she'd never been caught, never been traced, never left a signature. Corps had bounties on her. She ignored them.

"She's real?"

"Very. And expensive. But she's the only one who can crack Atlas encryption without triggering their countermeasures." Vera pulled up a holo-display. Floor plans. Security patterns. "Job's tomorrow night. You in?"

Kai considered. High-risk. Atlas. But if the data was medical tech, maybe it connected to the Surge. Maybe it was answers.

"Yeah. I'm in."

"Good. One more thing." Vera stood. "I'm not Juno. I'm harder, meaner, and I don't forgive mistakes. But I honor his network. You were his crew. That makes you mine now. Don't make me regret it."

She dropped creds on the table. Enough to cover drinks and then some.

"Meet me at the safehouse tomorrow. 1900 hours. I'll send coordinates." She paused at the door. "And Reyes? Visit RN. Juno would want you to."

Then she was gone.

Kai sat alone. Finished his drink.

Pulled up the Associates password on his Data Tap. Stared at it.

He'd go. Eventually.

Just not tonight.

---

The safehouse was in Hollow Circuit—underground network of abandoned subway tunnels converted into smuggler dens, black market hubs, and merc staging areas. VSD didn't patrol here. Too dangerous. Too deep. Too much effort for too little payoff.

Vera's coordinates led Kai to a reinforced door twenty meters below street level. Passcode accepted. Door opened.

Inside: Compact operations room. Weapon racks, medical supplies, tactical displays, secure comms. Professional setup.

Two people waited.

The first was a woman—late twenties, sharp features, dark hair pulled back in a practical style. Augmented eyes—not flashy, not Vantage quality, but functional. Data cables coiled at her wrists like sleeping serpents. Minimal other chrome. She dressed like someone who lived in shadows and liked it.

Elena Markovic. Had to be.

She looked up as Kai entered. Scanned him with those augmented eyes—assessing, cataloging, filing away. "You're Reyes. Heard about you."

"Good things?"

"You survived a Blackout Division ambush. That's not luck. That's skill or stupidity." She tilted her head. "Haven't decided which yet."

"Skill."

"We'll see."

The second person stood in the corner. Silent. Still.

Woman, mid-twenties, Japanese features, athletic build. She wore dark practical clothing, nothing flashy. But Kai's eyes went to the weapon strapped to her back.

Katana. Traditional grip, well-maintained blade. Not decorative. A tool. A extension of self.

She hadn't moved when he entered. Hadn't acknowledged him. Just watched. Presence like a held breath.

"That's Ashira," Elena said. "She doesn't talk much."

Ashira's eyes met Kai's. Brief. Measuring. Then returned to watching the room.

Professional. Dangerous. Good.

Vera entered behind Kai. "Everyone here. Good. Let's talk business."

She activated the holo-display. Atlas facility blueprints appeared—three-story structure, heavy security, isolated location.

"Atlas R&D archive. Sector 12, industrial district. Minimal staff at night, automated security, hardened servers." Vera highlighted entry points. "Two guards at main entrance, cameras on rotation, pressure sensors on primary access routes."

"What's the target data?" Kai asked.

"Medical technology files. Prototype development, testing logs, classified project documentation. Specific file designations will be provided to Elena once we're inside."

Elena leaned forward. "Encryption level?"

"Military-grade. Atlas Blackwall protocols. You'll need to dive deep."

"How deep?"

"Deep enough that if you trigger countermeasures, you'll be brain-dead before we extract you."

Elena smiled. Not humor. Just acknowledgment of challenge. "My favorite kind of job."

Kai looked at Ashira. "You've worked with her before."

Ashira nodded once. Didn't elaborate.

Elena answered for her. "Ashira keeps me alive while I'm under. Someone has to watch my body when I'm in the Grid. She's good at it."

"And I'm here because?"

"Because two is better than one," Vera said. "Ashira handles close threats. You handle perimeter and backup. Elena needs both of you functional."

Made sense. Divers were vulnerable when diving—consciousness in the Grid, body a vegetable. Easy targets.

"Payment?" Kai asked.

"Ten thousand creds. Each. Half up front, half on completion."

Good money. Very good. "I'm in."

"Figured." Vera transferred five thousand to his Data Tap. "We move at 2200 hours. Gear up. Stay sharp. And don't fuck this up."

---

2200 hours. Industrial district. Atlas R&D archive.

The building squatted like a concrete tumor—minimal windows, reinforced walls, security sensors bristling from every angle. Corps built their secrets to last.

Kai, Elena, and Ashira approached from the maintenance access—service tunnel that ran beneath the structure, minimal surveillance, Vera's intel said it was viable.

It was.

They breached quietly. Elena's toolkit made short work of the access lock. Inside: maintenance corridors, dim lighting, the hum of ventilation systems.

Elena moved like she belonged here. Confident. Knew the layout from blueprints, walked it like memory. Ashira followed—silent, katana loose in its sheath, eyes tracking every shadow.

Kai took rear guard. Vantage Optics scanning for thermals, motion, threats.

They reached the server room. Elena knelt beside the main terminal. Pulled cables from her wrists, jacked into the port.

Her eyes went distant. Unfocused. She was in.

"How long?" Kai whispered.

Ashira held up ten fingers. Ten minutes. Maybe.

Kai and Ashira took positions—Ashira at the door, Kai covering the corridor. Waiting.

Kai's Vantage picked up movement. Two floors up. Guards on patrol. Standard route.

He subvocalized into comms. "Two contacts. Upper level. Not a threat yet."

Ashira acknowledged with a hand signal.

Time stretched. Five minutes. Six.

Elena's body twitched. Facial micro-expressions—stress, concentration, navigating hostile digital terrain.

The guards moved closer. Stairwell. Descending.

Kai's hand went to his pistol. "They're coming."

Ashira shifted. Ready.

Eight minutes.

The guards reached their floor. Flashlights sweeping corridor.

Kai made the call. Stepped into the hallway. Hands visible. Non-threatening.

"Hey. Lost. Looking for the bathroom?"

The guards raised weapons. "This is a restricted area! Hands up!"

Kai complied. Bought seconds.

Then Ashira moved.

Fast. Inhumanly fast. Katana cleared its sheath like liquid metal.

First guard didn't see her coming. Blade took his rifle, not his throat—disarmed, not killed. Professional. Controlled.

Second guard tried to fire. Ashira's foot caught his wrist. Gun clattered. Follow-up strike—pommel to temple. He dropped.

Five seconds. Two guards neutralized. Non-lethal.

Kai stared. "You're fast."

Ashira said nothing. Just sheathed her blade. Returned to position.

Elena gasped. Pulled cables free. Eyes focused. "Got it. Let's move."

They extracted. Fast. Clean. No additional contacts.

Outside, three blocks away, Vera waited with transport.

"Status?"

"Data acquired. Two guards disabled. No casualties. Clean exit." Elena handed over a data chip. "Files are encrypted. Client will need the decryption key I'm sending separately."

Vera transferred the second half of payment. "Good work. All of you."

Kai checked his account. Ten thousand creds. Biggest single payday since the war.

"We're done?" he asked.

"For now. I'll reach out if I need you again." Vera looked at him. "And Reyes? Don't forget. RN. Sector 7. Wall 3."

He nodded. "I won't."

---

Kai went alone.

Late evening. The Remembrance Nexus was quiet—families visiting during day hours, only a few stragglers now. The building was respectful. Dignified. Clean lines, soft lighting, none of the corpo glitz that infected everything else in Veyline.

Independent operation. Community-funded. The only place in the city where death was treated with dignity, not profit.

Kai found Sector 7. Wall 3. Plaque 47.

**JUNO CROSS**

**CONTRACTOR • FATHER • BROTHER**

**2041 - 2082**

Simple. Clean. True.

Kai pressed his Data Tap to the neural port embedded in the plaque. Connection established. HUD displayed options.

```
MEMORY THREADS AVAILABLE:
> PUBLIC: 12 threads
> ASSOCIATES: 5 threads [PASSWORD REQUIRED]
> FAMILY: 8 threads [LOCKED]

> ENTER ASSOCIATES PASSWORD?

```

He entered Vera's password. Access granted.

```
> DOWNLOAD AUDIO THREADS? [Y/N]

```

Yes.

Files transferred. Kai unplugged. Walked.

Through Edgehaven. Past vendors closing for the night. Through streets he'd walked a hundred times. Plugged in his earpiece. Played the first thread.

**VERA CROSS:**

> "Juno Cross. Contractor. Brother. Father. You kept the code to the end. Emma and Lena are safe. I'll make sure of it. Network's taking care of them—father would be proud. You went out protecting your crew. That's the legacy. Rest well, little brother."
> 

Professional. Controlled. But Kai heard the crack underneath. The sister grieving a brother while maintaining the code.

Next thread.

**MARCUS KANE:**

> "One of the best, Juno. Never broke contract, never sold out. Word's out about the Blackout ambush—your crew survived because of you. That's what matters. Contractor's oath honored. See you on the other side."
> 

Next.

**TAKESHI MORI:**

> "Added five thousand to Emma's fund. Your girl will have what she needs. You went out right, brother. Atlas can't take that from you."
> 

More threads. More Contractors. All saying the same thing in different words: *You were good. You kept the code. We remember.*

Then Dax's voice.

**DAX VEGA:**

> "Juno... it's Dax. I was driving that day. I got Kai out. I couldn't get you. I'm sorry. I think about that every day—if I'd been faster, if I'd seen it coming. But I know you'd tell me to stop. You made your call. You protected your crew. I'll remember that. I'll try to live up to it. Rest well, brother."
> 

Kai stopped walking. Stood in an empty street.

Dax carried that guilt too. They all did.

He kept walking. Passed a residential block. Recognized it.

Juno's street. His building.

Kai slowed. Looked up.

The apartment was dark. Empty. Moving notices still taped to the door—weeks old, weathered.

They'd left. Couldn't stay where Juno's ghost lived in every corner.

Kai stood there. Stared at the dark windows.

*Your family moved on because you're gone. Your daughter asks about you. Your wife can't stop grieving. And I'm alive because you died.*

He turned away. Kept walking.

Finished the threads. All of them. Contractors honoring one of their own. The code. The network. The thing that bound them when everything else was broken.

Kai returned to RN. Found Juno's plaque again.

Saw two figures at the memorial. Vera. And a small girl—seven years old, dark hair, holding flowers.

Emma.

Vera was kneeling beside her. Talking quietly. Emma nodded. Placed the flowers at the plaque's base.

Kai stayed back. Shadows. Watching.

Emma said something. Too far to hear. Vera hugged her.

They stayed for several minutes. Then left.

Kai waited until they were gone. Approached.

The flowers were fresh. Cheap, but carefully arranged. A child's offering.

He pressed his Data Tap to the plaque again. Selected: **LEAVE MEMORY THREAD.**

Typed:

> Juno, it's Kai Reyes. I was with you that day. I'm sorry I couldn't bring you home. I'm sorry your last thought was your daughter and you never got to see her again. You died doing your job, protecting your crew. That's worth something. I hope.
> 
> 
> *Your family is being taken care of. The network is honoring you. Emma will know her father was a good man. I promise I'll make sure of that.*
> 
> *I'm alive because of you. I won't waste it.*
> 
> *Rest well.*
> 

Sent.

The plaque glowed softly. Message logged.

Kai stood there. Hands in pockets. Pendant cold against his chest.

Thought of his own brother. The pendant's original owner. Lost in Ashen Reach.

Two brothers. Two debts. Both unpayable.

But he'd try anyway.

Kai turned. Walked away from the memorial.

Behind him, Juno's plaque stood silent. Flowers at its base. Threads of memory linking the dead to the living.

The only place in Veyline where death was treated with dignity.

The only place where the dead were still remembered.

Kai walked into the night. Carrying debts he couldn't repay.

But carrying them forward anyway.
